WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.899
Film Courage: How does someone know which story they
should write? Kelli McNeil, Writer, Actress and Voice Over Artist: you should write the one you're

00:00:04.899 --> 00:00:09.850
afraid to tell. If it scares you,
then you know you need to do it. I

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:15.010
have a couple like that right now that
I'm working on I have a play that is

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:20.380
going to be going up off-broadway next
year it was it was actually one of the

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.740
very first pieces of writing I had ever
done I was in a group called the Los

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.330
Angeles writers centre for years when I
was kind of dipping my toe into writing

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:32.140
and decided I wasn't very good at it but
I had written to play

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:39.190
it's a two-act play and I wrote it in
2007 and it's actually scary how how

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:45.239
applicable it is to today's current
climate it's about a Mexican-American

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:50.710
soldier who's injured in Iraq and his
illegal brother has died while they're

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.940
serving over there. So this was right
after 2001 and he comes back to Los

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.149
Angeles and he discovers that his two
younger siblings have been put into

00:00:58.149 --> 00:01:02.739
foster care and his parents have deport
been deported and I wrote this in 2007

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:08.940
oh yeah and it was it was it's called
borderline and it was actually a

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.660
semifinalist at AFF last year again as
soon as as soon as I started to build up

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.260
my own confidence in my writing I took
up some stuff and I dusted them off and

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.600
I started to submit them out and I'm a
friend of mine who I went to USC with

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:28.450
who runs a theater up in Manhattan and
this is a issue that is that she's very

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:32.770
passionate about and I've never
connected the dots before but I sent it

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:36.610
to her and she said do you want to I
said do you want to read this and she

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:40.300
said yeah absolutely so she read it she
goes why don't we do a reading so we did

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:45.460
a reading in April this year in New York
and we have decided to workshop it and

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:51.280
with the intent of putting it up in the
fall of 2020 now I have not done any of

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.470
the rewrites yet because I am silently
criticizing myself because how do I take

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:00.970
the enormity of everything that's going
on right now and put it into a two-act

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:05.800
play and again you know why am I the
person to tell this story and this is

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:12.310
something that I wrestled with and I I
hope it comes out well I hope I tell an

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:15.670
authentic story
and I hope I create something that

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:19.930
really resonates and move people but my
job as a writer is to really take myself

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:24.670
in this particular instance is to take
myself out of it and to put my fear

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:30.700
society you know behind me so I can so I
can draft the piece we need writers

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.350
right now more than ever because a lot
of people's voices are being quieted and

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:41.250
this is not the time does it scare you

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.840
yeah absolutely
why does it scare you tell that's um you

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:53.320
know I certainly don't want anything to
ring inauthentic or Hollow and of course

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.550
you know if I'm if I'm telling a story
that doesn't necessarily relate to you

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:02.140
know my own upbringing you know my
family he's not immigrants I mean

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:06.670
they're they're from Italy but that was
two generations ago so I wasn't directly

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:10.420
impacted by that going up but there are
a lot of people that are hurting right

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:15.850
now there are a lot of people that are
struggling so for me to have to you know

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:20.380
that's a lot of weight to carry on my
shoulders but I put that weight upon

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.739
myself because I feel like this is a
very important story to tell and it's

00:03:23.739 --> 00:03:28.540
one that I want to tell so I'm doing a
lot of research right now I'm speaking

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:33.250
with immigration attorneys I am reading
you know news articles I'm doing a ton

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:39.400
of research it's it's my job to tell the
story the best that I can I mean I'm

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:45.730
crafting the story as well but I find
you know this is this other piece that I

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:51.910
worked on Daruma this this particular
story it it starts to disabled leads but

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:56.800
the movie is not about their disability
and I I wrote this as a way to deal with

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.959
my own grief after a member of my family
became paralyzed in an accident now the

00:04:01.959 --> 00:04:06.100
story is not about them it's it's not
about the melody but it's inspired by

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:13.540
them and I went through dozens of drafts
of that script because I wanted to tell

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:19.209
an authentic story now I'm not a
disabled person I the last thing I

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:23.010
wanted to do was to put anything on the
page that was you know inauthentic

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.509
offensive
I put that script through so many

00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:31.280
sensitivity reads which is a which is an
important thing to do as a writer and

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.530
that's not to say I away from telling
you know racy material or things you

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:41.990
know that that are taboo definitely tell
those stories but it's important in this

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:46.129
level of heightened awareness to to put
your things through a sensitivity read

00:04:46.129 --> 00:04:51.199
and I changed things about that script I
I took things out you know one thing

00:04:51.199 --> 00:04:58.520
that I I kicked myself for are one of
our leads John in the script his his

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:06.050
character salt is Robert but Robert is a
double arm amputee and I wrote Robert

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:13.039
how I thought a double arm amputee would
be a very limited action very limited

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:19.550
movement and then we cast John Lawson
and my god that guy he is a private

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:25.759
pilot he can scuba dive he's an amazing
photographer as a writer when I

00:05:25.759 --> 00:05:31.339
initially did I I limited him and it
took me meeting John and interacting and

00:05:31.339 --> 00:05:36.589
really ingratiating myself at that
community to open myself up and that's

00:05:36.589 --> 00:05:43.370
how you tell an authentic story what is
the sensitivity read so a sensitivity

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:49.190
read is to put your material in front of
you know a person that you are writing

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:54.080
about so let's just say that you're
writing about a disabled person or maybe

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.550
you're writing about somebody who has
HIV or maybe you're writing about an

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:02.210
immigrant maybe you're writing about
somebody with topical issues you know

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:06.969
and anything that can be considered
emotionally or politically charged so

00:06:06.969 --> 00:06:15.009
dealing with issues of race color
economic class anything that can be

00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:19.159
positive
possibly disparaging to a particular

00:06:19.159 --> 00:06:27.229
group of people it's important that you
share those materials you you you put it

00:06:27.229 --> 00:06:32.659
in front of you know readers and you do
a table read you know do we have actors

00:06:32.659 --> 00:06:37.970
read the parts back to you and you know
there was in my play border

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:42.470
one of the feedback one of the pieces of
feedback that I'm working into I have

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:47.330
sort of a Greek chorus of Dia de los
Muertos characters and of course that's

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:53.800
you know that's something that's very
traditionally you know Mexican they're

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:58.760
in in my story they function as a Greek
chorus and they're kind of ominous

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:04.510
figures but I had a Latino cast and you
know two out of three of them were like

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:08.540
you really need to be a little bit more
positive with these guys because you

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:12.830
know the Dia de los Muertos is a way to
honor family and tradition and these

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:17.870
guys kind of come across as as baddies
and I'm like okay I didn't know that so

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:21.490
I was a writer you need to be open to
hearing those kinds of suggestions

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:26.180
because you know every everybody knows
the stories you know cowboys and Indians

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:31.910
you know what would a classically
machismo you know imperialistic way of

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:39.530
thinking and nothing would make that
movie these days it's it's it wouldn't

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:43.610
happen you know you go back and you look
at a lot of movies from your childhood

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:48.590
or than 90 xoring a lot of these raunchy
comedies they're creepy you know if you

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:54.830
you look at a lot of movies now like
especially in the 80s like that's rape

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:59.180
culture and that perpetuates things you
know people consume the media that you

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:06.860
write it's an it's important to have
people look at your materials to make

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:12.140
sure it kind of passes a smell test now
that's that's a that's a sensitivity

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:17.539
read interesting and what about the
criticism that somebody who hasn't been

00:08:17.539 --> 00:08:21.140
through something shouldn't be allowed
to tell it I feel like in this climate

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:25.190
because somebody's voice may be censored
or they may be afraid to tell that story

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:29.510
that somebody else who may feel like I
don't know Society might view them as

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:33.409
they have more freedoms to tell a story
should be able to then get that story

00:08:33.409 --> 00:08:40.610
out there I have to two things to say
about that the director of my play the

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:46.580
stage reading that we did she's Latin
acts amazing young auteur we had a

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:49.550
conversation you know we were getting
ready for pre-production

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:53.300
she asked me she said you are UV was
Hispanic descent I said no I'm not she

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:55.430
was what your Spanish that's pretty good
and I was like well I worked with the

00:08:55.430 --> 00:09:00.529
translator so to get that going and she
asked me she goes why are you the one to

00:09:00.529 --> 00:09:03.529
tell the story she's like you know you
were a white woman why are you the one

00:09:03.529 --> 00:09:12.440
to to tell this story and my answer was
that no one else is telling it no one

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:16.700
else is telling this story and you know
regardless of my background or my skin

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:20.899
color or anything I live in this world
and these are issues that I care about

00:09:20.899 --> 00:09:25.100
so if I care about them enough to try to
talk about them and to try to push them

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:29.990
out into the world you know my my story
may not be the end-all be-all you know

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:33.890
you know end of the subject matter it's
that's not going to be the case but this

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:38.570
is something that I care deeply about
and I you know I live in this world

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:43.760
I'm impacted by these issues too so
that's that's one way that I address

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.649
those particular questions I had a
general a couple weeks ago with another

00:09:48.649 --> 00:09:53.810
